{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on J4 test continuation (fc614759): a new full gate at the final head; the follow-up exception does not cover a regenerated production module\n\nDecision. Regenerate apps/room/page/assets.ts with the maintained build, commit it with its explanatory documentation, and run the whole gate once at that final head. The targeted follow-up exception in docs/testing.md (\"Follow-up after a gate\") permits changes to the failing test file and explanatory documentation only; production code must remain unchanged. assets.ts is the module the scope Worker serves at /page/, so the repair is a production change, and the exception is closed to it. This is the same answer as ruling 31962e1f on the evidence-rules slice, where two regenerated modules took the head outside the exception.\n\nConditions, as in 31962e1f.\n1. The gate runs at the final head. A later commit means the gate runs again at the new head, unless that commit changes only documents (two tree hashes stated).\n2. The evidence table keeps the failed gate at 40230449 (two seal cases) and the failed continuation at e9da1ba8 (assets.test.ts) as separate rows with their exit codes and output digests. The seal owner's 10/10 follow-up stays its own row. None of these becomes a pass; the full gate at the final head is the pass or the failure.\n3. The note names the regeneration command (npm run build --workspace @generalbusiness/dap-page) and shows that running it at the final head reproduces the committed bytes: 1,204,952 bytes, SHA256 a859bd6bf1af271585fb17179e3f182a53a25c9a88bd0d2dc5db8cb9653c3b67, or whatever the build at the final head produces, and that assets.test.ts passes against it.\n4. The note states what changed in the bundle (the capability code that the Page imports) in one sentence, so the reader knows why the generated module moved.\n5. No stage result from the failed runs stands in for the gate's Counting, plain or Native phase. The checker assesses the final gate, and reads the failed rows as history.\n\nWhy not the narrower route. The three unreached stages after the seal follow-up are part of the same failed gate, and the gate's purpose is one observed run of everything at the head under review. Two partial runs stitched across two heads do not give the checker that observation, and the cost of one gate is small against a J4 landing. Retire this question when read.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}